To find the best fit for therapy, it's essential to consider your specific needs and goals. Since you're looking to improve your emotional security and self-confidence, you may want to explore therapies that focus on building self-awareness, emotional regulation, and coping skills. 

Cognitive-Behavioral Therapy (CBT) and Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT) are both evidence-based approaches that can be helpful in addressing emotional insecurity and building self-confidence. CBT focuses on identifying and challenging negative thought patterns, while DBT emphasizes the development of emotional regulation and coping skills.

You may also want to consider humanistic or psychodynamic therapies, which focus on personal growth, self-awareness, and exploring the underlying causes of your emotions and behaviors.

When searching for a therapist, look for someone who specializes in the areas you're interested in (e.g., anxiety, self-esteem, relationships) and has experience working with clients with similar concerns. Many therapists offer a free initial consultation, which can be a great opportunity to discuss your needs and see if you feel comfortable with their approach.

Remember, the most important factor in successful therapy is the therapeutic relationship – finding a therapist with whom you feel a strong rapport and trust is crucial. Don't be afraid to ask questions, and don't hesitate to try out a few different therapists before finding the right fit for you.

